
// draft
split_into(iterable: Int[], sizes: Option<Int>[]) -> Int[][]
	note "cut the iterable into consecutive chunks, one per size, a None size taking everything that remains"
	# If no size is None, the output has exactly one chunk for each size, in the order of the sizes.
	len(out) == len(sizes) or (any s in sizes: s == None)
	# If some size is None, the output has one chunk for each size up to and including the first
	# None, and none for the sizes after it.
	all s in sizes where s == None: any t in sizes[len(out) - 1 .. len(out)] where t == None: none u in sizes[0 .. len(out) - 1]: u == None
	# The chunks joined in order are the items of the iterable from its start, in the iterable's
	# order, with no item skipped or repeated.
	flatten(out) == iterable[0 .. len(flatten(out))]
	# No chunk is longer than the whole-number size it was cut for.
	// reaching for: for every position i in out, if sizes at position i is a whole number n, then
	// the length of the chunk at position i of out is at most n. That needs the chunk and the size
	// at the same position, a positional index or a zip of out with sizes, and the fragment has
	// neither.
	# If a chunk is shorter than its whole-number size, the chunks joined in order are the whole
	# iterable.
	// reaching for: if, at some position i, sizes at i is a whole number n and the chunk at
	// position i of out has length less than n, then flatten(out) == iterable. Like the line above,
	// it pairs each chunk with the size at its own position, which needs a positional index or a
	// zip of out with sizes.
	# If some size is None, the chunks joined in order are the whole iterable.
	flatten(out) == iterable or (none s in sizes: s == None)
	// example wanted: iterable [1,2,3,4,5,6], sizes [1,2,3]: the sizes sum exactly to the length,
	// so every chunk is full
	// example wanted: iterable [1,2,3,4,5,6], sizes [2,3]: the sizes sum to less than the length,
	// so the tail is not returned
	// example wanted: iterable [1,2,3,4], sizes [1,2,3,4]: the third chunk overruns the iterable
	// and the fourth comes back empty
	// example wanted: iterable [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,0], sizes [2,3,None]: the None chunk takes
	// everything that remains
	// example wanted: iterable [1,2,3,4,5], sizes [2,None,1]: the size after the first None gets no
	// chunk at all
	// example wanted: iterable [1,2,3], sizes []: no sizes, so no chunks
	// example wanted: iterable [], sizes [2,None]: every chunk comes back empty, including the None
	// chunk
	// example wanted: iterable [1,2,3], sizes [1,0,2]: a size of 0 is an empty chunk in the middle
	// and uses up nothing
	// example wanted: iterable [1,2,3], sizes [-1]: islice refuses a negative size; the author
	// decides whether this is an error case or a refinement on sizes
